<BaseLayout title="About — HPCI Toolkit" description="About the HPCI Toolkit, the research behind it, and how to get in touch.">
  <section class="container prose">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      The HPCI Toolkit is an open-source kit for designing at the meeting point of
      humans, plants, and computers — treating vegetal life not as scenery or raw
      material, but as a participant in how we build technology. It grew out of my
      doctoral research at Trinity College Dublin.
    </p>

    <h2>The research</h2>
    <p>
      Human-Plant-Computer Interaction (HPCI) extends human-computer interaction to
      recognise plants as active participants in socio-technical systems, rather than
      passive data sources or background. Plants operate on different timescales, modes
      of communication, and logics of care, and taking them seriously unsettles some of
      the anthropocentric assumptions built into how we design. Part of what motivates
      the work is <em>plant blindness</em>: the tendency to overlook the ecological and
      cultural significance of plant life, even as ecological crisis and digital
      saturation make that neglect harder to justify.
    </p>
    <p>
      My research asks how we might design HPCI that fosters positive societal change.
      That means mapping HPCI as a field — its long, interdisciplinary history across
      art, agriculture, bioelectrical engineering, robotics, and more — and then opening
      it toward the <em>pluriverse</em>: Arturo Escobar's idea of "a world where many
      worlds fit." Dominant technology tends to encode a single way of knowing and an
      extractive relationship to knowledge, land, and non-human life. Drawing on
      pluriversal thinking, Indigenous Data Sovereignty, and the principle of
      <em>buen vivir</em>, I explore what it looks like to design instead for reciprocity,
      regeneration, and more-than-human agency — the expanded space of relations between
      human, plant, and computational intelligence that runs through all of this work.
    </p>
    <p>
      Methodologically, I treat speculative design and fiction as generative tools rather
      than predictions — ways to rehearse the values, risks, and possibilities of systems
      still in formation — alongside research through design and participatory, backcasting
      workshops. The toolkit is the practical outcome of a series of prototypes and studies:
      <em>FloraFictions</em>, a co-creative VR workshop for telling more-than-human stories
      through virtual plant life; <em>The Whispering Greenhouse</em>, an augmented tabletop
      role-playing game where real plants become portals into speculative worlds;
      <em>A.I.gdals</em>, a speculative model of seasonal, community-governed AI inspired by
      Amazigh Agdal practices in Morocco; a reading of the animated series
      <em>Scavengers Reign</em> as a speculative prototype for HPCI; and
      <em>Roots of Evolution</em>, an immersive VR journey through plant evolution.
    </p>

    <h2>The toolkit</h2>
    <p>
      The HPCI Toolkit is the open-source, hands-on answer to that research: a modular kit
      for building systems in which a plant — or a whole environment, like a forest or a
      garden bed — a person, and a computer stay in ongoing relationship. It's designed to
      be picked up in workshops and taken apart, modified, and repurposed. Its logic rests
      on a few simple pieces: a <strong>named subject</strong> you are in relationship with;
      <strong>sensing</strong>, which lets the plant's signals enter the system;
      <strong>rituals</strong> — repeatable, meaningful choreographies of tending and
      attending rather than one-off commands; a <strong>journal</strong> that keeps a record
      of the relationship over time; and a shared <strong>Commons</strong> where observations
      can be pooled. Underneath sits an architecture built around three agents — plant, human,
      and computer — and the relationships that move between them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two of my own projects show what repurposing the toolkit can look like.
      <strong>Veyra</strong> is a tabletop role-playing game that takes the toolkit in a
      storytelling and game direction, turning plant sensing and ritual into the mechanics of
      shared narrative play. <strong>The Ritual Grove</strong> is its more practical
      counterpart, using the same backbone for biodiversity and rewilding — a way of
      structuring attention, observation, and care around a living site over time. Both are
      meant as invitations: examples of how the toolkit can branch into very different worlds.
    </p>

    <h2>Who I am</h2>
    <p>
      I'm Nour Boulahcen, a doctoral researcher at Trinity College Dublin working on
      Human-Plant-Computer Interaction and more-than-human design. My work sits between HCI,
      speculative and interactive narrative, and ecological thought, and is shaped by my own
      familial and cultural ties to Morocco — which is part of why questions of cultural
      plurality, Indigenous knowledge, and design beyond WEIRD contexts run through it. You
      can find my publications and academic profile on
      {' '}
      <a href="https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3863-3199" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ORCID</a>.
    </p>

